    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `customerCode` assignment (`<LAST3>-<NNNN>`) on owner creation, keeping the change small and cohesive.\n\n**New unit \u2014 the actual behavior** (`AssignCustomerCode.java`): a single-method function class (no surrounding class complexity to hold) that runs before `save` in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline. It takes the first three letters of `lastName` upper-cased, and a 4-digit sequence = `ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1`. Because it runs before the save, the sequence excludes the owner being created, so consecutive creates increment by exactly one (verified by the test's `ca + 1 == cb` assertion).\n\n**Wiring & plumbing** (small, no complex functions touched):\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted an `assignCode` step between `ensureUnique` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 added a `customerCode` field plus a trivial getter/setter; MapStruct then maps it into `OwnerDto` by name automatically (no mapper edit needed).\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 declared `customerCode` on the `Owner` schema as `readOnly`.\n- `db/hsqldb/schema.sql` \u2014 added the `customer_code` column (hsqldb is the test profile with `ddl-auto=none`; h2 uses `ddl-auto=update` so it needs no schema edit).\n- `db/hsqldb/data.sql` \u2014 appended a trailing `NULL` to the 10 positional owner seed inserts to match the new column count.\n\nI avoided growing any existing method: the logic lives in a brand-new one-method class (WMC_other \u2248 1), and the only edits to the large `Owner` class are new trivial accessors rather than changes to complex existing methods.",
